2. Limitations of Classical Research Architectures

2.1 Human-Bounded Cognition

Traditional research workflows rely on unaided human reasoning augmented only by passive computational tools. Cognitive synthesis, hypothesis generation, and validation are sequential and slow.

2.2 High Iteration Cost

Experimentation pipelines often span months or years. Failed assumptions can invalidate entire research programs, discouraging speculative or integrative inquiry.

2.3 Compute Scarcity and Centralization

High-performance computing has historically been restricted to governments, large corporations, or elite institutions, reinforcing asymmetries in innovation capacity.

2.4 Knowledge Fragmentation

Biology, physics, computation, and social systems evolve in parallel silos, inhibiting holistic system-level understanding.

2.5 Externalized Governance

Ethical review, safety evaluation, and societal impact assessment are typically applied after technical development, rather than embedded within the system itself.

